From 6bb1511f8377781989bb5cca61893e6a70143051 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.local>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 15:02:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix word-boundary matching bugs in the assistant intent
 parser
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

parseAssistantIntent() used a plain normalized.includes(intent) to match
workflow trigger phrases against the customer's message - the same class
of bug already fixed in the informational assistant's classifier
(assistant.service.ts). Short intent triggers misfired on ordinary words
that merely contain them as a substring fragment:
- 'nin' (NIN slip lookup) matched inside "training"
- 'data' (data purchase) matched inside "database"
- worst case: ipe_clearance's trigger was hand-written as 'ipe ' (with a
  literal trailing space) as an incomplete boundary workaround - this
  still matched inside "swipe " or "recipe ", while ALSO failing to
  match "ipe" typed as the very last word of a message with nothing
  after it (no trailing space to match against at all).

Fix - phraseMatches() in assistant-workflow.service.ts:
- Unicode-aware boundary matching ((?<![\p{L}\p{N}])phrase(?![\p{L}\p{N}]))
  rather than \b, since \b only understands ASCII word characters and
  would misbehave around Hausa letters like ƙ/ɗ/ɓ that may appear in
  future intent phrases.
- Replaces the workflow-matching .includes() call, and ipe_clearance's
  intent list drops the 'ipe ' trailing-space hack in favor of a clean
  'ipe' now that boundaries are handled properly on both sides.
- Also applied \b to the adjacent dataType regex (corporate/coupon/share/
  gift/sme detection), which had the same unbounded-substring shape -
  'corporate' could misfire inside "incorporated".

Testing (76 new tests, assistant-workflow.service.test.ts):
- phraseMatches() unit tests for the boundary logic itself.
- Regression tests reproducing each historical false positive above
  ("training", "database", "swipe", "recipe") alongside confirmation
  that real requests - including the fixed 'ipe' typed alone - still work.
- A mechanically-generated self-consistency audit: every single intent
  phrase across all 14 workflows in the catalog, sent alone, must resolve
  back to the workflow that owns it - same permanent-regression-guard
  pattern used for the informational assistant's own classifier audit.
  Also a duplicate-phrase-across-workflows check.
- Full backend suite: 155/155 passing.
---
